.TH TERMIMAGE 1 "August 25, 2026" "TUI" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
termimage \- show images in the terminal, as pixels or as coloured characters
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B termimage.py
.RI [ options ]
.I image
.br
.B termimage.py
.RI [ options ]
.B \-\-wiki
.I topic
.br
.B termimage.py \-\-probe
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B termimage
displays a picture inside a terminal window. It has two completely different
ways of doing that, and by default it works out which one your terminal can
handle and uses the better one.
.PP
The
.I image
argument is either a path to a local file or an
.B http://
or
.B https://
URL. With
.B \-\-wiki
the picture is instead the lead image of a Wikipedia article, fetched by
title.
.PP
PNG is handled directly. Other formats (JPEG, WebP, and so on) are converted
first using
.BR sips (1),
which is part of macOS.
.SH THE TWO APPROACHES
.SS Approach B: real pixels
The terminal is sent the image file itself and draws it with actual pixels,
using the kitty graphics protocol. This looks like a photograph, because it is
one. It requires a terminal that implements the protocol \(en kitty, Ghostty,
WezTerm and Konsole do; Terminal.app does not.
.SS Approach A: coloured characters
Each character cell is filled with
.RB \(lq \[u2580] \(rq
(upper half block), with the foreground colour set to the top pixel and the
background colour to the bottom one. That gives two pixels per cell, so a
40-column picture is 40 by 60-odd pixels. It looks like a coloured mosaic, and
it works in every terminal that supports 24-bit colour, including over
.BR ssh (1)
and inside
.BR tmux (1).
.PP
If the terminal cannot do 24-bit colour either, brightness is mapped onto the
characters
.B " .:\-=+*#%@"
instead, which needs nothing but ASCII.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-mode " auto\fR|\fPkitty\fR|\fPblocks\fR|\fPascii\fR|\fPboth"
Which rendering to use.
.B auto
(the default) asks the terminal what it supports and picks the best available.
.B both
draws the picture twice, once each way, with labels \(en the quickest way to
see what your terminal is capable of.
.TP
.BI \-\-width " cells"
How many columns wide to draw the picture. The height follows from the image's
aspect ratio. Defaults to 60, and is capped to the width of the window.
.TP
.BI \-\-wiki " topic"
Use the lead image of this Wikipedia article. The article title is printed
above the picture.
.TP
.BI \-\-lang " code"
Language edition to use with
.BR \-\-wiki .
Defaults to
.BR en .
.TP
.BI \-\-bg " rrggbb"
Background colour composited underneath transparent parts of the image, as six
hex digits. Defaults to
.BR 000000 .
Only affects the character modes.
.TP
.BI \-\-transfer " auto\fR|\fPdirect\fR|\fPfile"
How the image reaches a kitty-capable terminal.
.B file
writes a temporary file and sends only its path, which is a hundred-byte escape
sequence rather than megabytes of base64; the terminal deletes the file itself.
.B direct
encodes the image inline in 4 KiB chunks, which is what you need when the
terminal is at the other end of an
.BR ssh (1)
connection.
.B auto
(the default) picks
.B direct
when
.B SSH_CONNECTION
is set and
.B file
otherwise.
.TP
.B \-\-probe
Print what this terminal supports \(en its size in cells, the pixel size of one
cell, whether the kitty graphics protocol answered, whether
.BR sips (1)
is present, and which mode would be chosen \(en then exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
Find out what your terminal can do:
.PP
.RS
.nf
termimage.py \-\-probe
.fi
.RE
.PP
See both renderings of the same picture, one above the other:
.PP
.RS
.nf
termimage.py cat.png \-\-mode both
.fi
.RE
.PP
Show a Wikipedia article's picture, 40 columns wide:
.PP
.RS
.nf
termimage.py \-\-wiki "Ada Lovelace" \-\-width 40
.fi
.RE
.PP
Force the character mosaic even on a terminal that could do better, and
composite transparency onto a dark grey instead of black:
.PP
.RS
.nf
termimage.py logo.png \-\-mode blocks \-\-bg 101010
.fi
.RE
.PP
Look at a picture on a remote machine over ssh:
.PP
.RS
.nf
termimage.py photo.jpg \-\-transfer direct
.fi
.RE
.SH HOW SUPPORT IS DETECTED
.B termimage
does not guess from
.BR $TERM .
It sends the terminal a graphics query and, immediately behind it, a standard
device attributes query, so that there is always a reply on its way. If the
device attributes answer arrives without a graphics answer, the terminal does
not speak the protocol and a character mode is used instead. The whole
exchange takes well under a second, and falls back safely if the terminal says
nothing at all.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The image was displayed.
.TP
.B 1
The image could not be read, fetched, or decoded; the reason is printed to
standard error.
.SH NOTES
The built-in PNG decoder handles every still PNG: all five colour types, bit
depths of 1, 2, 4, 8 and 16, every scanline filter, and Adam7 interlacing.
This matters because
.BR sips (1)
preserves interlacing and bit depth when it converts a PNG to a PNG, so it
cannot be used to rescue those two cases.
.PP
Animated formats are not supported; the first frame is not extracted.
.PP
With
.BR \-\-transfer " file"
the temporary file is deleted by the terminal, not by
.BR termimage .
If you force
.BI \-\-mode " kitty"
on a terminal that does not support the protocol, that file will be left behind
in the temporary directory until the system cleans it up.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wiki_tui.py (1),
.BR wiki_reader.py (1),
.BR sips (1)
.PP
The kitty graphics protocol is documented at
.UR https://sw.kovidgoyal.net/kitty/graphics\-protocol/
sw.kovidgoyal.net
.UE .
